{"step_index":65,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T05:04:55Z","input_tokens":3772,"cache_read_tokens":140710,"output_tokens":1931,"thinking":"I'm matching the ghost boxes in frame 700 against the V5_FencePosts positions, and the top heights line up with a fence post top around 4.4 m, though I'm not sure whether the dimensions run from 1.0 to 4.4. Several of the boxes are heavily defocused, so I'm judging them by their approximate top y-values and x-positions.\n\nI'm checking whether these ghost boxes are an intended result of making the fence posts visible, since the spec asked for real thin posts and the earlier renders showed none. Blurred by the depth of field, they read as translucent rectangles in the sky, which could count as stray boxes against the \"no stray boxes\" criterion. I'm also wondering whether a catch-fence net was meant to sit between the posts, since that would change how they should look.\n\nI'm weighing whether the wide translucent bands at frame 700 are an acceptable side effect of the fence post fix. The depth of field from the 78 mm lens at f/5.6 blurs posts sitting 64 to 113 m away, so they spread into wide ghostly strips. That's a consequence of a requested bug fix, so I'm leaning toward passing it with a note for the coordinator, with thinner or shorter posts as an option if they want it softer.\n\nWorking through the numbers, the depth-of-field estimate gives only about 1.3 px of blur for these posts, far too small to explain 40 to 70 px wide boxes. So the smearing is more likely motion blur from the fast-panning camera, which would also explain the yellow ad board on the left looking similarly stretched in the reference frame.\n\nI'm double-checking whether the fence posts are genuinely thin quads, since 972 faces across 486 posts suggests two faces per post, and a flat quad viewed along the track would be nearly invisible. That seems fine for now.\n\nI'm going to check the fence post timings by running the first part of the animation script in a fresh Blender session without saving, since that avoids altering anything.\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"$p=\"C:\\Users\\86052\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\16b5f3cb-4bce-43d1-a09a-ebf98bd665b7\\scratch\\v_physics.py\"; $src=Get-Content d:\\项目session\\blender-supercar\\pipeline\\v5_anim.py -Encoding UTF8; ($src[0..304] -join \"`n\") | Out-File -Encoding utf8 $p; Select-String -Path $p -Pattern \"save_as|open_mainfile|OUT\" | ForEach-Object { \"$($_.LineNumber): $($_.Line)\" }","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Extract physics check portion"}}]}
